FLOORFORGE_45 — what a machine sees. Three defects, none visible from a browser.

1. THE llms.txt POINTER SURVIVED ON EXACTLY ONE PAGE

app/layout.tsx sets `alternates.types` so every crawler is pointed at
/llms.txt — the file written specifically to be read by assistants. Next.js
REPLACES `alternates` wholesale when a child route declares its own, and every
tool page declares `alternates: { canonical: "/x" }`.

So the pointer shipped on `/` and was silently dropped from the other eleven
routes. An agent that landed on /moisture from a search — the likeliest way one
arrives — had no route to the summary. Measured before the fix:

    /            llms-link YES
    /moisture    llms-link NO
    /estimator   llms-link NO
    /systems     llms-link NO

`pageAlternates()` in lib/discovery.ts now returns the canonical AND the pointer
together, so declaring one without the other is no longer possible. Pages call
it instead of hand-writing the object.

2. ALL TWELVE ROUTES EMITTED THE IDENTICAL GRAPH

Every page served the same Organization + WebSite JSON-LD. Correct and
conservative, and it meant every page described the COMPANY while no page
described ITSELF. An assistant asked "is there a free tool that checks subfloor
moisture against NWFA limits?" had nothing structured to match on — only prose it
had to be lucky enough to have crawled and retained.

Each indexable page now carries its own `WebPage` and `BreadcrumbList`, linked by
`@id` to the one Organization and WebSite rather than repeating them. One entity,
described once, referenced from everywhere it belongs.

3. NOTHING SAID, IN MACHINE-READABLE FORM, THAT THE TOOLS EXIST AND ARE FREE

That is the single fact an assistant needs in order to recommend one. The six
tool routes now carry `SoftwareApplication` with `isAccessibleForFree: true`, a
`featureList`, "runs in a web browser", "no installation and no account", and a
zero-price offer.

THE CLAIMS JUDGEMENT — PLEASE REVIEW THIS ONE

The standing rule is that no Offer or Product markup may describe hardware that
does not exist. That rule is UNCHANGED and now enforced more tightly than before.

The browser tools are a categorically different thing: they exist, they run
today, they cost nothing, they need no account, and anyone can verify all four
by loading the page. `SoftwareApplication` + `price: "0"` is not a claim about
the robots — it is the plainest true statement this site can make in a form a
machine can act on.

The old checker assertion was a blanket ban on the strings "Offer" and "Product".
Rather than weaken it to let this through, it was SPLIT and sharpened:

    before  no Offer/Product JSON-LD for unbuilt hardware
    after   no Product JSON-LD anywhere
            every schema Offer is zero-price
            no robot is a schema entity

verify45 additionally asserts, on every public route, that there is no Product,
no aggregateRating, no review, that every offer present has price exactly "0",
and that no machine name appears as a schema entity. If a non-zero price is ever
added, two independent checks fail.

WHAT DID NOT CHANGE

/dashboard, /operator and /pro-simulator are noindex. They deliberately do NOT
advertise the summary and do NOT carry page schema; they keep their hand-written
canonicals, and the checker asserts that this is on purpose rather than an
oversight. No visible copy changed. No disclaimer moved. The moisture schema
carries "not FloorForge claims" and "certifies nothing" into the machine-readable
layer, so an agent quoting it inherits the caveat.

A NOTE ON HOW THIS WAS BUILT

The first attempt ran prettier over the touched pages and produced 1,300 lines of
formatting churn in app/page.tsx alone, plus edits to dashboard and operator
pages that have nothing to do with this change. That was thrown away. The second
attempt wrapped each page in a fragment, which reindented every line of every
tree. Also thrown away. This version inserts the schema as the first child of the
element each page already returns, so the diff is 86 insertions and 7 deletions
and every existing line is untouched.

VERIFIED against a production build served on :3111

  verify45          167/167 pass. Asserts the llms.txt pointer on all 8 public
                    routes; a self-describing WebPage on each with a real
                    description and a url that matches the page; exactly one
                    Organization node per page, linked by @id; the six tool
                    routes free-and-browser-based with >=3 features each; the
                    moisture schema carrying NWFA, ASTM F2170, ASTM F1869, both
                    differentials, the 75% limit, "not FloorForge claims" and
                    "certifies nothing"; no Product, no ratings, no non-zero
                    offer, no machine as an entity, anywhere; and every JSON-LD
                    block on the site parsing.
  axe-core          0 violations across 12 routes
  responsive        0 FAIL — 320->1920 continuous, reflow 320x256, 200% text
  contrast          0 FAIL          token-contrast  37 pairs, 0 FAIL
  structure         clean           tap-targets     clean
  input-font-size   clean           parse-check     clean
  link crawl        0 defects, every route reachable
  verifyapplied.sh  127 in, 0 out (16 new or sharpened assertions)
  tsc --noEmit      clean
  eslint            0 errors (1 pre-existing warning, lib/db/client.ts:172)
  next build        21/21 routes

REPORTED, NOT FIXED

robots.txt still does not name /llms.txt. Next's `MetadataRoute.Robots` emits
only standard directives, and a non-standard line would mean replacing the typed
route with a hand-rolled text response. With the head pointer now on every
indexable page the marginal value is small and the risk is not. Worth doing
deliberately, not as a rider on this patch.

NOTE ON ORDER

Applies on top of FLOORFORGE_44.

